  [3mWins when[0m you care about the redaction actually being correct, which given the password-adjacent call sites you
  should.

  [1mB. Single-file universal logger[0m
  One [38;5;153msrc/logger.js[39m with the same dual-export footer, transports registered by the host at init time. Everything in ~120
  lines.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m simplest thing that works, and honestly proportionate to a repo this size. But core logic and runtime I/O
  live in one file, so testing redaction means loading the module that also installs global error handlers. That
  coupling is exactly what makes the security-critical part awkward to verify.
  [3mWins when[0m you want this done in one sitting and accept thinner tests.

  [1mC. Migrate the repo to native ESM[0m
  Convert [38;5;153msrc/*.js[39m off CommonJS, set [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m, switch [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, and use real
  [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m everywhere. Both Node and browsers support this natively — still no bundler.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m the cleanest module story, but it's a repo-wide migration riding along with a logging request, it changes
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m from global scope to module scope, and native ESM won't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m — so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly from
  disk stops working and you'd need a local server. That's a real regression bought with a change nobody asked for.
  [3mWins when[0m the module system is itself a problem you want to fix. It isn't yet.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m The allowlist is the one piece of this change that causes harm if it's subtly wrong, and A is the only
  option that lets you test it as a pure function in isolation. It costs you three extra small files and buys a
  verifiable security boundary, with no dependency and no build step.
